4. Critical Findings Before Porting

Critical: The current WebSocket design is a stateful multiplayer server. A stateless Worker port would be behaviorally incorrect even if it compiles.

Live connections, session ownership, heartbeat, pending broadcasts, and combat timers are process-local. Map each combat session ID to one Durable Object and persist resumable state deliberately.

Host authority is granted by client message shape and student identity is trusted from the client. Authenticate the WebSocket upgrade and bind host/student roles to verified principals before cutover.

Teacher authorization checks only for the presence of a teacher session in multiple places; ownership checks are incomplete. Fix authorization as a migration gate so the port does not preserve cross-tenant access flaws.

Test teacher, student, fight, guild, and combat-session records are seeded at every server start. Disable production test seeding immediately and move approved seeds to an explicit job.

API logging captures serialized response bodies. Replace it with structured, redacted logs before enabling Cloudflare observability.

The repository has no automated test script and no dedicated health endpoints. Create route-contract, session, WebSocket, storage, and smoke tests before behavior is split across services.

5. Target Architecture

Browser at questacademy.bookwyrminteractive.studio
  -> Cloudflare Pages: Vite SPA and immutable assets
  -> Pages Functions gateway: /api/*, /ws, /objects/*
       -> Service binding: QuestAcademy API Worker
            -> Neon PostgreSQL (optionally through cache-disabled Hyperdrive)
            -> Durable Object per combat session for WebSockets and timers
            -> GCS adapter or R2 binding for private objects

Request ownership

Pages owns static files and SPA navigation on the QuestAcademy hostname.

Pages Functions matches dynamic paths before fallback and forwards the original request over a Service binding. Unknown API paths return JSON 404, not index.html.

The API Worker owns REST, authentication, session persistence, database access, object policy, and health endpoints.

The Worker routes /ws upgrades to a Durable Object chosen from the verified combat session ID. The object owns the room; it is not a global singleton.

Neon remains the system of record for teachers, students, fights, guilds, completed combat results, and session records. Durable Object storage holds only state required for reliable live-session recovery.

Cookie and origin policy

Cookie name: replace the generic connect.sid with a product-specific name such as qa_session.

Attributes: Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax unless a proven cross-site flow requires otherwise; Path=/; no Domain attribute.

Use a 60-minute rolling expiry only if product owners confirm that current behavior. Logout must delete the same server row and clear the cookie using identical path/security attributes.

Use same-origin requests and WebSockets. Do not add permissive CORS. Preview origins should use separate staging sessions and data.

