7. Phased Migration Plan

The estimates below are planning ranges for one experienced engineer with timely product and account decisions. They are not release commitments. The security, live-combat, and storage findings are the schedule drivers.

Phase 0 - Decisions and inventory

Indicative effort: 1-2 engineering days

Approve the .studio subdomain as canonical and identify the owner of the apex DNS zone.

Inventory current production host, DNS records, Neon connection limits, object count/size/URLs, and active authentication flows.

Choose Pages + Worker (this plan) versus one Worker with Static Assets. Choose GCS adapter versus R2 migration.

Record acceptance owners, release window, traffic expectations, retention/compliance needs, and rollback authority.

Exit gate: Architecture decision record approved; unknowns have owners and due dates.

Phase 1 - Stabilize and secure the current Node app

Indicative effort: 3-6 days

Add route-contract tests for REST and WebSocket messages before refactoring.

Disable unconditional production test seeding; make approved seeds explicit and idempotent.

Add teacher resource-ownership checks, WebSocket authentication/role binding, rate limits, CSRF posture, and safer password comparison.

Add /api/health/live and /api/health/ready, request IDs, structured logs, and response-body redaction.

Introduce configuration and object-storage interfaces while keeping Replit behavior available for rollback.

Exit gate: Legacy deployment is safer, observable, testable, and still releasable.

Phase 2 - Cloudflare project foundation

Indicative effort: 2-4 days

Add build:client, build:worker, check, test, dev:cloudflare, and deploy scripts without replacing the legacy start script.

Create Pages configuration for dist/public and preview environments.

Create the API Worker configuration, compatibility date, secret declarations, Service binding contract, Durable Object migration stanza, and staging environment.

Add Pages Functions gateway routes for /api/*, /ws, and /objects/*; prove WebSocket upgrade forwarding in staging.

Add static headers: immutable caching for fingerprinted assets; revalidation/no-cache for HTML.

Exit gate: A hello-world SPA, API health route, and WebSocket echo pass on preview URLs.

Phase 3 - REST, Neon, and sessions

Indicative effort: 5-10 days

Port HTTP routes in vertical slices: health, read-only public routes, teacher auth/session, then authenticated mutations.

Use a Worker-supported Neon/Drizzle setup. If Hyperdrive is used, disable query caching for auth, sessions, permissions, and read-after-write flows.

Replace connect-pg-simple middleware with an explicit session repository and signed opaque cookie.

Run the same contract suite against legacy and staging; compare status, JSON shape, validation, and errors.

Keep database schema changes expand/contract and compatible with both hosts.

Exit gate: All REST and session acceptance tests pass against a staging Neon branch or isolated schema.

Phase 4 - Real-time combat on Durable Objects

Indicative effort: 8-15 days

Extract deterministic combat rules from Express/socket transport code.

Implement one Durable Object per session ID using Hibernation WebSockets; authenticate upgrade before joining.

Move room membership, host authority, heartbeat, broadcasts, reconnect dedupe, and timers to the object. Use alarms for durable deadlines.

Define checkpoints to Durable Object storage and Neon; ensure final result writes are idempotent.

Run multi-room, reconnect, eviction/restart, latency, timer-drift, and 2-30-player load tests. Avoid dual-active game writers during cutover.

Exit gate: A full teacher-hosted fight completes after forced object restart, with correct persisted results.

Phase 5 - Object storage

Indicative effort: 3-8 days plus copy time if R2

Inventory object operations, visibility, metadata, signed URL lifetime, CORS, range requests, and legacy /objects references.

For GCS retention: replace the Replit sidecar with Worker-compatible authentication/signing and fetch streaming.

For R2: provision staging/production buckets, implement an adapter, dual-read old/new, write-new, backfill, checksum, and sampled visual verification.

Persist object keys rather than host-specific URLs where practical; normalize legacy references at read time until migrated.

Exit gate: Uploads and existing images work from the canonical host; inventory and checksum reports reconcile.

Phase 6 - Staging acceptance and operational readiness

Indicative effort: 3-5 days

Run the complete acceptance matrix on Pages preview, staging custom hostname, desktop, and mobile.

Enable Workers logs/traces, dashboards, alert thresholds, and a release-day tailing procedure.

Rehearse Worker rollback, Pages rollback, Neon compatibility, Durable Object constraints, and storage fallbacks.

Perform security review of tenant boundaries, cookies, CSRF, WebSockets, uploads, caches, and secret handling.

Exit gate: Named acceptance owners sign off; rollback rehearsal is timed and documented.

Phase 7 - Controlled cutover

Indicative effort: 1-2 days plus observation window

Deploy immutable versions and smoke-test them before changing the custom domain.

Attach questacademy.bookwyrminteractive.studio only after DNS conflict review and certificate readiness.

Run immediate synthetic, teacher, student, combat, upload, and database smoke tests.

Observe errors, latency, WebSocket churn, Neon connections, and object failures. Keep the legacy deployment available.

After the acceptance window, optionally redirect the old /QuestAcademy address to the subdomain if that address exists and apex ownership permits it.

Exit gate: Production acceptance is complete; rollback window and legacy-retirement date are explicitly approved.

