12. Cutover Runbook

T-7 days: freeze destructive schema/storage changes; confirm acceptance owners and rollback operator; publish maintenance/launch communication if needed.

T-2 days: deploy final candidate versions without production traffic; run full staging acceptance and load tests; verify secrets/bindings and certificate readiness.

T-30 minutes: confirm legacy health, Neon backups/restore point, object fallback, dashboards, and a clean rollback target.

T0: attach or activate the QuestAcademy Pages custom domain. Do not modify apex records beyond the reviewed subdomain change.

T+5 minutes: test home, nested route, teacher login, student join, API mutation, WebSocket upgrade, one short combat, upload/read, and logout.

T+60 minutes: review HTTP errors, tail latency, Worker CPU/duration, WebSocket disconnects, Durable Object alarms, Neon connections/errors, and object failures.

T+24 hours: repeat representative teacher/student sessions and confirm no data divergence. Keep legacy host intact.

After the agreed observation window: enable the optional old-path redirect, document the stable Worker/Page versions, and schedule legacy retirement separately.

13. Rollback Plan

Rollback principle: Code can roll back quickly; database, Durable Object, and storage changes cannot. All state changes must remain backward compatible until the rollback window closes.

Trigger

Immediate action

State protection

Frontend failure

Roll back Pages deployment or detach custom domain to the approved prior destination.

No database change; invalidate only if a bad HTML shell is cached.

REST/auth failure

Roll back Worker version; keep Pages gateway binding stable; route users to legacy host if needed.

Both versions must understand current session/schema formats.

Combat failure

Stop new sessions on Cloudflare; let active legacy sessions finish or direct all new sessions to one system.

Never run two authoritative writers for one combat session.

Neon overload/error

Disable cutover or reduce traffic; restore legacy application path; investigate pool/cache settings.

Avoid emergency schema reversal; use expand/contract migrations.

Object failure

Switch adapter to GCS/legacy dual-read; pause uploads if integrity is uncertain.

Keep source objects and reconciliation manifest until acceptance closes.

Security incident

Disable affected route/custom domain, revoke/rotate secrets, invalidate sessions, preserve logs.

Follow incident process; do not conceal or overwrite evidence.

Cloudflare Worker rollback creates a new deployment from an earlier version, but it does not roll back Neon, R2, KV, or Durable Object data. Durable Object class lifecycle changes can also constrain rollback. Introduce the namespace/class before traffic cutover and avoid destructive migrations during the release window.

14. Open Decisions and Blockers

Confirm the canonical hostname spelling and whether the former /QuestAcademy address has ever been public.

Identify the current production host, its rollback URL, and the owner authorized to change the subdomain record/custom domain.

Approve Pages + Worker versus unified Workers Static Assets. Pages is the plan baseline; Workers Static Assets is Cloudflare current new-project recommendation.

Decide GCS retention versus R2 using the object inventory, egress cost, auth compatibility, operational ownership, and rollback needs.

Confirm whether teacher sessions must remain 60-minute rolling sessions and whether any cross-site callback requires SameSite=None.

Define real production use of WebSockets, expected concurrent sessions/players, latency target, and reconnect tolerance.

Choose acceptance-test owners, release window, monitoring thresholds, and legacy-host retirement criteria.

Confirm compliance/data-residency requirements for student data, logs, database, Durable Objects, and object storage.

